Sam Dennis wrote:

> I feel that the question that I attempted to answer was more likely to
> be the one intended than the one that you answered;

I'm not sure I agree.  For example, consider this parallel situation:
There is a slot machine that pays off with a probability of 1/100.  Your
friend, the Lucky Person (LP), walks up and says:

LP:  Guess what just happened!  I was playing the slot machine,
     and I won!  I was just going to take the winnings, but then
     I decided to play it just one more time.  And I won again!
     What are the chances of that?

Sam: Well, the chances are 1/100, given that you already won the
     first time.

LP:  Huh?  But 1/100 is the chance of just hitting it once.
     I won *twice in a row*, which obviously is more rare.
     What are the chances of _that_?

The 1/100 answer is correct, and may even help disavow the LP of the
Gambler's Fallacy, but it doesn't satisfy his desire to know how "rare"
this vaguely-defined "thing" that just happened is.  Since you are
giving him information he already knew, it seems he must be asking for
something else, or he wouldn't have posed the question.

Probably the best way to answer is to take into account the number of
times the machine is played.  As the number lowers, you approach my
answer, and as it increases, you approach yours (assuming you are
requiring the consecutive win to be directly after the very first win;
otherwise it approaches 1).

>>"That" could be getting the second wish after having gotten the first
>>one (your answer), ... or even something like "getting both wishes in
>>two consecutive #sits after any number of non-wish #sits" (a third
>>answer which no one has given; this interpretation also takes possible
>>pre-wish #sits into account in a fairly reasonable way).
> 
> AFAICS these two are equivilant, at least in this case.

Maybe I didn't state that clearly enough.  What I meant was, perhaps
this is the most useful way to approach the question:  You see a throne.
 You keep sitting at it until it either disappears, or you get two
wishes in a row.  What is the probability of getting the two wishes in a
row?

Calculating this over a few million trials, I got a value of 0.126% (if
you allow intervening single wishes), or 0.124% (if you don't).  Since
the throne tends to disappear relatively quickly, this comes out closer
to the "next two #sits are wishes" probability of 0.0438% than the "wish
on next #sit given first wish" probability of 1.71%.


